.TH BOSWAS-CONTROL-CENTER 1 "November 2026" "boswas-control-center 3.0~alpha1" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-control-center \- settings hub of Boswas OS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-control-center
.RB [ \-\-page
.IR page
.RB [ \-\-section
.IR section ]]
.br
.B boswas-control-center
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-control-center
is Boswas Control Center, the settings hub of Boswas OS. It is laid out in
categories: the pane on the left holds Home, System, Bluetooth & devices,
Network & internet, Personalization, Apps, Accounts, Time & language,
Accessibility, Privacy & security and Boswas Update. A category lists its
pages as cards; the path above the content shows where you are (for example
System > Display), and a crumb or the back button (Alt+Left) returns. Each
page shows the state of the device as the system reports it and holds the
settings of its area as native sections: rows with a switch, a list or a
slider that apply at once and show a message next to the control when a
change could not be made. No page depends on a program of another desktop.
.PP
Home shows the device (its name, kind and model, with Rename), the person
signed in, the network connection, Boswas Update, recommended settings
(dark mode and night light as switches, Display and Sound), the Bluetooth
devices, the background and theme, and how full the storage is.
.PP
The pages: System: Display, Sound, Power & battery, Storage, Troubleshoot,
Recovery, About, and in a live session Install Boswas OS. Bluetooth &
devices: Bluetooth, Printers, Cameras, Mouse, Touchpad, Keyboard, USB,
Hardware & Drivers. Network & internet: Wi\-Fi, Ethernet, VPN, Airplane
mode, Proxy, Advanced network settings. Personalization: Background,
Colours, Themes (the Boswas presets), Fonts. Apps: Installed apps, Software
Center, Default apps, Startup, Optional features, Windows Compatibility,
Android. Accounts: Your info & other users, Access work or school. Time &
language: Date & time, Language & region. Accessibility: Visual effects,
Mouse pointer. Privacy & security: Boswas Security Center, Lock screen, App
permissions, Activity history, Diagnostics & feedback. Boswas Update: the
update status and pending updates, Update history, Advanced options. A page
that has nothing to show on a system has no card.
.PP
Only one Control Center runs in a session. Starting the program again does
not open a second window: the running window comes to the front and shows
the place (and section) the new command line names. The quick settings of
the shell use this, for example
.B boswas\-control\-center \-\-page network \-\-section wi\-fi
for "More Wi\-Fi settings", which opens Network & internet > Wi\-Fi.
.PP
Typing in the search box above the categories lists the categories, pages,
sections and single settings whose names or keywords contain every word;
a result opens its place and scrolls to its section, and Enter opens the
first result.
.SS Settings sections
Network, Bluetooth, Sound and Power talk to the system through
.IR /usr/libexec/boswas/boswas\-services\-bridge ,
the same code the shell's quick settings use (NetworkManager, BlueZ, the
sound server, UPower, power\-profiles\-daemon and the backlight), so both
always show the same state. One bridge runs per window, from the first time
such a page is opened until the window closes. A Wi\-Fi passphrase is
written to the bridge's standard input only; it is never part of a command
line and never logged.
.PP
Display, Devices (mouse, touchpad, keyboard), the power and lock time\-outs
and the appearance settings are the compositor's: Control Center writes the
user's layer of its configuration,
.I ~/.config/boswas/compositor.toml
and
.IR ~/.config/boswas/appearance.toml ,
and asks the compositor to load it again; see
.BR boswas\-compositor (1).
.PP
Date and time and Language and region (Time & language) use
.BR timedatectl (1)
and
.BR localectl (1).
For changes that need administrator rights systemd's services ask polkit
themselves, and the shell's agent shows the question.
.PP
Users shows the account you are signed in with (picture, name, password)
and the other people's accounts, and adds and removes users, through the
AccountsService. It is asked with
.BR busctl (1)
for everything that is not a secret. Your own password is changed by
.BR passwd (1)
on a pseudo\-terminal, so the system's password rules apply; another
user's password is given to the service as a SHA\-512 crypt hash over
D\-Bus. No password and no password hash is ever part of a command line.
The account you are signed in with and the last administrator cannot be
removed. Without the AccountsService the accounts of
.I /etc/passwd
are shown and only your own password can be changed.
Default applications are written to
.I ~/.config/mimeapps.list
and start\-up applications to
.IR ~/.config/autostart .
.PP
The Security Center shows the local posture checks of
.B boswas \-\-json status
and the state of the Boswas device agent. Each item is shown as Secure,
Warning, Attention, Info, Not available or Error, always as text and as an
icon. The checks are a local self\-assessment of the device, not remote
attestation.
.PP
The Update Center shows whether automatic security updates are enabled and
when they last ran, and lists the pending updates (an APT simulation from the
package lists on the device, marking security updates). Check for Updates
refreshes the package lists and Install Updates upgrades the packages (never
removing any); both are administrator operations.
.PP
The Software Center lists the installed applications (Boswas, Windows and
the other desktop applications) and opens native applications with
.B gio launch
(see
.BR gio (1)).
Its other tabs search and install Debian packages from the signed
repositories, install Flatpak applications for the user from remotes the
administrator enabled, add AppImages (copied to ~/Applications; they run
without a sandbox), and route a chosen file by its content: Debian packages
to the package manager (with a warning that their origin is not verified),
Windows programs to the Compatibility Manager, Android apps to the Android
page.
.PP
The Android page shows the optional Android runtime (Waydroid) as reported by
.BR boswas\-android (1):
compatibility checks, installation, initialisation, start, stop, repair,
update and removal, the installed Android apps, and installing an APK file
after it was inspected. Google Play is shown as the runtime manager assesses
it; F\-Droid is offered as an alternative under its own name.
.PP
Hardware & Drivers lists the PCI and USB devices with the driver each one
uses (from sysfs; serial numbers are never read) and checks the kernel log for
missing firmware, offering to install the Debian package that provides it.
Storage shows the disks and file systems and frees space (package cache,
system journal, thumbnail cache) and opens the file system in Files
.RB ( boswas\-files (1)).
Its cleanup recommendations list the biggest items of the cache folder, the
Trash (freedesktop Trash specification) and the downloads not changed for
14 to 90 days; only what the person ticks is cleared, the Trash is emptied
after a question, and old downloads are moved to the Trash, not deleted.
.PP
About includes a system overview (load, memory, network, runtimes) and
renames the device:
.B hostnamectl \-\-static set\-hostname
.I name
(1 to 63 letters, digits and hyphens, not starting or ending with one);
systemd\-hostnamed asks polkit. Troubleshoot runs, on request, read\-only
checks of the internet connection (an adapter, an address, a route, name
servers, then a name lookup of deb.debian.org and an HTTPS request to it,
which the page says before it runs), the sound, Bluetooth and the
monitors, each with what helps. Recovery restarts into the firmware
settings or the boot loader's menu
.RB ( "systemctl reboot \-\-firmware\-setup" ,
.BR "\-\-boot\-loader\-menu=0" )
only where logind says the device supports it, after a question.
.PP
Printers lists the printers of CUPS
.RB ( lpstat ),
prints CUPS's own test page
.RB ( "lp \-d" " printer " /usr/share/cups/data/testprint )
and, without CUPS, installs the package cups through the system helper.
Cameras lists the cameras of /sys/class/video4linux and opens the Camera
application; USB lists the connected USB devices. VPN lists the VPN and
WireGuard connections of NetworkManager, connects, disconnects and removes
them and imports a WireGuard or OpenVPN file the person chooses
.RB ( "nmcli connection" ...);
no secret is ever part of a command line. Advanced network settings shows
each adapter with the data it moved since the device started, its hardware
address when asked, and turns it off and on
.RB ( "nmcli device disconnect" | connect ).
.PP
Fonts lists the font families
.RB ( fc\-list ),
previews them, installs a TrueType or OpenType file for the user (checked
by its header, at most 64 MB, copied to
.I ~/.local/share/fonts
and followed by
.BR "fc\-cache \-f" )
and removes fonts the user installed. Installed apps lists every application
with its origin (the owning Debian package from
.BR "dpkg\-query \-S" ,
Flatpak, AppImage, Windows) and removes it the way it was installed.
Optional features installs and removes optional Debian packages (codecs,
printing, fonts, Firefox in the languages of this device) through the
system helper. App permissions lists what sandboxed (Flatpak) applications
were allowed through the desktop portals
.RB ( "flatpak permission\-list" )
and revokes a decision
.RB ( "flatpak permission\-remove" ).
Update history reads
.I /var/log/apt/history.log
and its rotated files.
.SH SECURITY
Control Center runs as the desktop user and never as root. It starts only
the programs it knows (boswas, boswas\-preset, boswas\-android,
boswas\-files, boswas\-compat\-manager, boswas\-camera, gio launch,
apt\-config, apt\-get (simulation only), apt\-cache, dpkg\-query (reading
only), flatpak, nmcli (queries, and the connection and adapter changes
above), ip (queries only), lsblk, timedatectl, localectl, hostnamectl (the
static host name only), locale \-a, busctl (calls to the AccountsService
and two read\-only questions to logind), passwd (without arguments),
systemctl (restarting, and whether Bluetooth runs), fc\-list, fc\-cache (the
user's font folder), lpstat, lp (CUPS's test page only), and the polkit
authorization tool), each with a validated argument list and a
timeout, and never through a shell; and the services bridge, by its fixed
path and without arguments. The only files it writes are listed under
FILES.
.PP
Administrator operations go only to the Boswas system helper
.I /usr/libexec/boswas/sysops
(package boswas\-sysops), with an operation from a fixed list; polkit asks
for authorization. Nothing privileged runs when the window opens or a page
is shown: every change starts with a click and a confirmation that says what
will change. Success is reported only when the helper reports success.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " page"
Open the window at
.IR page ,
a category: home, system, devices, network, personalization, apps,
accounts, time\-language, accessibility, privacy\-security or updates; or a
page: display, sound, power, storage, troubleshoot, recovery, about,
install (live sessions only), bluetooth, printers, cameras, mouse, touchpad,
keyboard, usb, hardware, wifi, ethernet, vpn, airplane, proxy,
advanced\-network, background, colours, themes, fonts, installed\-apps,
applications (the Software Center), default\-apps, startup,
optional\-features, windows, android, users, work, date\-time, region,
visual\-effects, mouse\-pointer, security, lock\-screen, app\-permissions,
recent\-files, privacy (Diagnostics & feedback), update\-history or
update\-options. The names of earlier versions still work: system,
devices, network and personalization open their category, and with
.B \-\-section
the section is looked for where it moved (for example
.B \-\-page system \-\-section date\-time
opens Time & language > Date & time).
.TP
.BI \-\-section " section"
With
.BR \-\-page :
scroll to this settings section of the page. A section is named by its
title in lower case with dashes, for example
.BR wi\-fi ,
.B night\-light
or
.BR date\-time .
A name the page does not have is ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYBOARD
.TP
.B Up, Down
In the category list: switch categories.
.TP
.B Alt+Left
Back to the place shown before.
.TP
.B F5
Refresh the current page.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F
Search the installed applications.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit (an operation that cannot be interrupted safely finishes first).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/boswas\-agent/agent.sock
The Boswas device agent, used read\-only for the device, policy, privacy and
runtime status.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas/session.sock
The user's Boswas session service, used read\-only for the Windows
applications and the compatibility runtime.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/boswas/release
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/boswas/image\-info
The Boswas OS version and build shown on the About page.
.TP
.I ~/Applications
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/applications/boswas\-appimage\-*.desktop
AppImages added in the Software Center and their menu entries.
.TP
.I ~/.config/boswas/compositor.toml
.TQ
.I ~/.config/boswas/appearance.toml
The user's layer of the compositor's settings (display, input, time\-outs,
appearance). Written atomically with mode 0600; never through a symbolic
link.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mimeapps.list
Default applications.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/*.desktop
Start\-up applications the user added, and the files that switch off a
system\-wide one.
.TP
.I ~/.face
The account picture (a PNG of 256 by 256 pixels), also given to the
AccountsService.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/recently\-used.xbel
The shared list of recently used files, emptied by "Clear" on Privacy &
security > Activity history.
.TP
.I ~/.cache
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/Trash
.TQ
.I ~/Downloads
Items of the cache folder the person picked are deleted, the Trash is
emptied, picked downloads are moved to the Trash (Storage).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/fonts
Fonts the user installed on the Fonts page.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas\-fluent/com.boswas.ControlCenter.sock
Where the running window listens for a second start of the program.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the window was closed normally, and when a running Control Center took
over; 2 for a wrong option; 4 when started as root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas (1),
.BR boswas\-android (1),
.BR boswas\-compat\-manager (1),
